{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Two tenons for one board.</b> He would cut away the board from below at its middle, in the height of a cubit, leaving a quarter of its width on this side and a quarter of its width on that side — and these are the tenons. He cut out half the width of the board in the middle, and those tenons he inserts into the sockets, which were hollow. The sockets were a cubit high and sat set close together, forty next to forty. The tenons of the board that enter the hollow of the sockets are cut out on three of their sides — the width of the cutout is like the thickness of the socket's lip — so that the board covers the entire top of the socket; for if not, there would be a gap between board and board like the thickness of the lips of the two sockets that intervene between them. And this is what is said: ״וְיִהְיוּ תֹאֲמִים מִלְּמַטָּה״ — that he should cut the sides of the tenons so that the boards join one to the other." "<b>Joined.</b> Made in the manner of the rungs of a ladder, separated one from the other, and their heads tapered to enter into the hollow of the socket — like a rung that enters into the hole of the ladder's uprights." "<b>Each woman to her sister.</b> Aligned one opposite the other, so that their cutouts are equal one to the other, lest the two tenons of one be pulled toward the inside and those of the other pulled toward the outside across the thickness of the board, which is a cubit. And the Targum of יָדוֹת is צִירִין — because they resemble door hinges that enter into the holes of the threshold."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:54:47.477773782Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}